Subject: [PATCH] router2, xilinx: partition pins for routing regions

A Region gains partition_pins, net name -> the wire at which the net
crosses the region's edge.  router2 holds an arc that crosses the edge
of such a region at its net's pin to the region on the inside of the
pin, provided the route on the outside is already locked: an arc into
the region is searched from the pin to its sink, an arc out of it from
its source to the pin, and the locked route on the far side is added to
the arc when the search is done.  The region rules and the seed filter
look only at the part inside.  An arc whose outside route does not reach
the pin as locked routing is routed as before, with a warning.

router1's final check no longer counts the route to a partition pin as
a stub: a module may leave a port unused, and the net then ends there.

xilinx: -o partition-pins=<file> ("<net>\t<wire>" per line) names the
pins.  They are read before -o prerouted, which now keeps a reference
route that ends at a pin instead of pruning it, and are given to the
pblock of the net's driver or sinks (or, for a net that no longer
enters a pblock, to the pblock whose pips the pin reaches).  Also in
apply_prerouted: a sink of a net route_clocks routed first that the
reference route does not reach keeps route_clocks' dedicated route from
where it joins the reference tree, instead of going to the general
router (which could take it through a LUT).

Base: router-region (e62fafd1) + nextpnr-pblock-replay.patch +
nextpnr-pblock-routethru.patch.  Not committed, not posted.
